IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `gaq-common`
IMPORT `gaq-administrator`

-- Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 (Queensland) -- worked cases for gaq-administrator.l4 (ss 37, 47, 48, 54,
-- 58-60C, 152, 153).
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Expected values were written by hand
-- from the deposited text (act-2000-008, in force from 2026-04-28), never copied from the encoding's output. No scenario
-- life raised this Act; the facts are the encoder's, HYPOTHETICAL throughout.
--
-- A breached DEONTIC cannot be compared with EQUALS on the stable l4, so the ss 59(5)-(6) and 60C(4) rules are asserted
-- through the questions that use CONSIDER ... WHEN FULFILLED, and one breach of each is #TRACEd with an "-- expect:" line.
--
-- Unofficial version. Based on content from the Queensland Legislation website at 6 November 2026. (c) The State of
-- Queensland, CC BY 4.0. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

GIVEN d IS A NUMBER, m IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`dmy` d m y MEANS DATE_FROM_DMY d m y

§ `ss 37, 152 -- conflict transactions`

-- Argument order: may conflict, only the s 37(3) carve-outs, s 54 gift or s 55 dependants, authorised before, after.
#ASSERT `Is my administrator's transaction allowed question` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `it is not a conflict transaction: s 37 does not restrict it`
-- A parent-administrator dealing with a house held jointly with the injured adult (s 37(3)(c)).
#ASSERT `Is my administrator's transaction allowed question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `it is not a conflict transaction: s 37 does not restrict it`
#ASSERT `Is my administrator's transaction allowed question` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `it is not a conflict transaction: s 37 does not restrict it`
#ASSERT `Is my administrator's transaction allowed question` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `it is a conflict transaction the tribunal authorised beforehand: allowed (ss 37(1), 152(1))`
#ASSERT `Is my administrator's transaction allowed question` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `it is a conflict transaction the tribunal authorised beforehand: allowed (ss 37(1), 152(1))`
#ASSERT `Is my administrator's transaction allowed question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `it is a conflict transaction the tribunal authorised afterwards: valid as if authorised beforehand (s 152(2), (3))`
-- Example 2 of s 37: the administrator lends the adult's compensation payout to a close friend, unauthorised.
#ASSERT `Is my administrator's transaction allowed question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `it is a conflict transaction without the tribunal's authorisation: your administrator has acted contrary to s 37(1) (s 152(4)); you may seek compensation or an account of profits (s 59)`

§ `ss 47, 48 -- paying the administrator`

#ASSERT `Can my administrator be paid from my money question` `reimbursement of expenses` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: reasonable expenses incurred in acting as your administrator are reimbursed from you (s 47)`
#ASSERT `Can my administrator be paid from my money question` `reimbursement of expenses` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 47 reimburses only reasonable expenses incurred in acting as your administrator`
#ASSERT `Can my administrator be paid from my money question` `remuneration for their work` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the tribunal has ordered remuneration, capped at what it considers fair and reasonable (s 48(1), (3))`
#ASSERT `Can my administrator be paid from my money question` `remuneration for their work` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: remuneration is payable only if the tribunal orders it (s 48(1))`
-- A family-member administrator with no professional business cannot be remunerated, order or not.
#ASSERT `Can my administrator be paid from my money question` `remuneration for their work` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the tribunal may order remuneration only for an administrator who carries on a business providing professional services (s 48(2))`

§ `s 54 -- gifts`

#ASSERT `Can my administrator give away my property question` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: a gift of a kind you made or might reasonably be expected to make, of a reasonable value; the administrator may even receive it (s 54(1), (2))`
#ASSERT `Can my administrator give away my property question` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the gift is not of a kind you made when you had capacity or might reasonably be expected to make (s 54(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can my administrator give away my property question` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the value is more than is reasonable, having regard in particular to your financial circumstances (s 54(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `Can my administrator give away my property question` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the tribunal's order governs: s 54(1) applies unless the tribunal orders otherwise`

§ `ss 58, 59(1)-(4) -- compensation from the administrator`

#ASSERT `Can I get compensation from my administrator question` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the tribunal or a court may order the administrator to compensate your loss, or to account for their profits, but not both for the same exercise of power (s 59(1), (2)); this holds even if the appointment has ended or they were convicted (s 59(3), (4)); a court may relieve an administrator who acted honestly and reasonably (s 58); an amount paid counts against damages in a later civil claim (s 59(9))`
#ASSERT `Can I get compensation from my administrator question` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the failure caused you no loss and brought the administrator no profit, so there is nothing for s 59 to order`
#ASSERT `Can I get compensation from my administrator question` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 59 needs a failure by the administrator to comply with the Act in exercising a power`

§ `s 59(5)-(7) -- the time to apply`

-- Argument order: adult died, day, administrator died, day.
#ASSERT `Last day to apply for compensation from my administrator question` FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `Last day to apply for compensation from my administrator question` TRUE (`dmy` 10 1 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) EQUALS JUST (`dmy` 10 7 2027)
-- Both died: 6 months from the first death (the administrator's, 5 December 2026).
#ASSERT `Last day to apply for compensation from my administrator question` TRUE (`dmy` 10 1 2027) TRUE (`dmy` 5 12 2026) EQUALS JUST (`dmy` 5 6 2027)
#ASSERT `Last day to apply for compensation from my administrator question` TRUE (`dmy` 1 3 2027) TRUE (`dmy` 1 4 2027) EQUALS JUST (`dmy` 1 9 2027)
-- The administrator died on 31 August 2027: 6 months on is February 2028, which has 29 days.
#ASSERT `Last day to apply for compensation from my administrator question` FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) TRUE (`dmy` 31 8 2027) EQUALS JUST (`dmy` 29 2 2028)

-- Argument order: adult died, day, administrator died, day, applied, day applied, extended, today.
#ASSERT `Is my compensation application in time question` FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) TRUE (`dmy` 1 6 2030) FALSE (`dmy` 1 6 2030) EQUALS `s 59 sets no time limit while you and your administrator are both alive`
#ASSERT `Is my compensation application in time question` TRUE (`dmy` 10 1 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) TRUE (`dmy` 10 7 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 8 2027) EQUALS `yes: made within 6 months after the death (s 59(5), (6))`
#ASSERT `Is my compensation application in time question` TRUE (`dmy` 10 1 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) TRUE (`dmy` 10 1 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 8 2027) EQUALS `yes: made within 6 months after the death (s 59(5), (6))`
#ASSERT `Is my compensation application in time question` TRUE (`dmy` 10 1 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) TRUE (`dmy` 11 7 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 8 2027) EQUALS `out of time: the 6 months after the death have ended; ask the tribunal or court to extend the time (s 59(7))`
#ASSERT `Is my compensation application in time question` TRUE (`dmy` 10 1 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) TRUE (`dmy` 11 7 2027) TRUE (`dmy` 1 8 2027) EQUALS `in time only because the tribunal or court extended the time (s 59(7))`
#ASSERT `Is my compensation application in time question` TRUE (`dmy` 10 1 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) FALSE (`dmy` 10 7 2027) EQUALS `not yet due: the 6 months after the death have not ended (s 59(5), (6))`
#ASSERT `Is my compensation application in time question` TRUE (`dmy` 10 1 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) FALSE (`dmy` 11 7 2027) EQUALS `out of time: the 6 months after the death have ended; ask the tribunal or court to extend the time (s 59(7))`
-- Both died: the clock runs from the first death, so an application in time for the second death can still be late.
#ASSERT `Is my compensation application in time question` TRUE (`dmy` 10 1 2027) TRUE (`dmy` 5 12 2026) TRUE (`dmy` 6 6 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 8 2027) EQUALS `out of time: the 6 months after the death have ended; ask the tribunal or court to extend the time (s 59(7))`
#ASSERT `Is my compensation application in time question` TRUE (`dmy` 10 1 2027) TRUE (`dmy` 5 12 2026) TRUE (`dmy` 5 6 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 8 2027) EQUALS `yes: made within 6 months after the death (s 59(5), (6))`
#ASSERT `Is my compensation application in time question` FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) TRUE (`dmy` 31 8 2027) TRUE (`dmy` 29 2 2028) FALSE (`dmy` 1 3 2028) EQUALS `yes: made within 6 months after the death (s 59(5), (6))`

-- 181 days allowed (10 January to 10 July 2027); nothing done by day 182.
#TRACE `s 59(5), (6) -- apply within 6 months after the death` 181 AT 0 WITH
    (WAITUNTIL 182)
-- expect: BREACH by the applicant, "s 59(5), (6): the application was not made within 6 months after the death ..."

§ `s 60 -- a lost benefit in the estate`

#ASSERT `Can I claim for a lost benefit in the estate question` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes: you may apply to the Supreme Court for compensation out of the adult's estate, of no more than the value of the lost benefit; Succession Act 1981 ss 41 and 44 apply to the application (s 60(3)-(5))`
#ASSERT `Can I claim for a lost benefit in the estate question` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 60 applies only to a benefit under the will, on intestacy, or by another disposition taking effect on the adult's death (s 60(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I claim for a lost benefit in the estate question` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: s 60 applies only where the benefit was lost because of a sale or other dealing with the adult's property by the adult's administrator (s 60(1))`
#ASSERT `Most compensation for a lost benefit question` 50000 40000 EQUALS 40000
#ASSERT `Most compensation for a lost benefit question` 50000 50000 EQUALS 50000
#ASSERT `Most compensation for a lost benefit question` 50000 50001 EQUALS 50000
#ASSERT `Most compensation for a lost benefit question` 50000 60000 EQUALS 50000

§ `ss 60A, 60C -- the proceeds`

#ASSERT `Can I apply about the proceeds question` `a beneficiary under the adult's will` EQUALS `yes: you have the same interest in the proceeds of the sale or dealing (with their traceable income and capital gain) as you had in the property, and may apply to the Supreme Court under s 60C within 6 months after the adult's death (ss 60A(2), (3), 60C(2), (4))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply about the proceeds question` `the personal representative of a deceased beneficiary under the will` EQUALS `yes: you may apply to the Supreme Court under s 60C within 6 months after the adult's death (s 60C(2), (4))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply about the proceeds question` `the adult's personal representative` EQUALS `yes: you may apply to the Supreme Court under s 60C within 6 months after the adult's death (s 60C(2), (4))`
#ASSERT `Can I apply about the proceeds question` `someone else` EQUALS `no: only a beneficiary under the will, a deceased beneficiary's personal representative, or the adult's personal representative may apply (s 60C(2))`
-- Died 31 August 2026: 6 months on is February 2027 (28 days).
#ASSERT `Last day to apply about the proceeds question` (`dmy` 31 8 2026) EQUALS `dmy` 28 2 2027
#ASSERT `Last day to apply about the proceeds question` (`dmy` 15 12 2026) EQUALS `dmy` 15 6 2027
-- Argument order: died, applied, day applied, extended, today.
#ASSERT `Is my application about the proceeds in time question` (`dmy` 31 8 2026) TRUE (`dmy` 28 2 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 4 2027) EQUALS `yes: made within 6 months after the adult's death (s 60C(4))`
#ASSERT `Is my application about the proceeds in time question` (`dmy` 31 8 2026) TRUE (`dmy` 1 3 2027) FALSE (`dmy` 1 4 2027) EQUALS `out of time: the 6 months after the adult's death have ended; ask the court to extend the time (s 60C(5))`
#ASSERT `Is my application about the proceeds in time question` (`dmy` 31 8 2026) TRUE (`dmy` 1 3 2027) TRUE (`dmy` 1 4 2027) EQUALS `in time only because the court extended the time (s 60C(5))`
#ASSERT `Is my application about the proceeds in time question` (`dmy` 31 8 2026) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) FALSE (`dmy` 28 2 2027) EQUALS `not yet due: the 6 months after the adult's death have not ended (s 60C(4))`
#ASSERT `Is my application about the proceeds in time question` (`dmy` 31 8 2026) FALSE (`dmy` 1 1 2000) FALSE (`dmy` 1 3 2027) EQUALS `out of time: the 6 months after the adult's death have ended; ask the court to extend the time (s 60C(5))`

-- 181 days allowed (31 August 2026 to 28 February 2027); nothing done by day 182.
#TRACE `s 60C(4) -- apply within 6 months after the adult's death` 181 AT 0 WITH
    (WAITUNTIL 182)
-- expect: BREACH by the applicant, "s 60C(4): the application was not made within 6 months after the adult's death ..."

§ `s 153 -- accounts and audit`

#ASSERT `Can I get my administrator's accounts question` `the adult (me)` EQUALS `yes: you may ask the tribunal to order your administrator to file accounts and serve them on you, and to have them audited with the report given to you, even if the appointment has ended or the adult has died (s 153(1)-(4))`
#ASSERT `Can I get my administrator's accounts question` `an interested person for the adult` EQUALS `yes: you may ask the tribunal to order your administrator to file accounts and serve them on you, and to have them audited with the report given to you, even if the appointment has ended or the adult has died (s 153(1)-(4))`
#ASSERT `Can I get my administrator's accounts question` `someone else` EQUALS `no: only the adult or an interested person may apply; the tribunal may still act on its own initiative (s 153(3))`
